msgid ""
msgstr ""
"# Fused Softmax Example\n"
"\n"
"This example demonstrates how to use the fused softmax kernel in FlashAttention. The fused softmax kernel combines the softmax computation with the preceding matrix multiplication (QK^T) and subsequent scaling operations, avoiding the need to write intermediate results to HBM (High Bandwidth Memory), thereby significantly improving performance.\n"
"\n"
"## Background\n"
"\n"
"In the standard attention mechanism, the computation process is as follows:\n"
"\n"
"1. Compute the attention scores: S = QK^T\n"
"2. Scale the scores: S = S / √d\n"
"3. Apply softmax: P = softmax(S)\n"
"4. Compute the output: O = PV\n"
"\n"
"In the traditional implementation, each step requires reading from and writing to HBM, which is very time-consuming. The fused softmax kernel combines all these steps into a single kernel, keeping the intermediate results in the on-chip SRAM, thus avoiding multiple HBM accesses.\n"
"\n"
"## Code Example\n"
"\n"
"```python\n"
"import torch\n"
"import flash_attn\n"
"\n"
"# Create input tensors\n"
"batch_size = 32\n"
"seq_len = 128\n"
"num_heads = 8\n"
"head_dim = 64\n"
"\n"
"# Randomly initialize query, key, and value tensors\n"
"q = torch.randn(batch_size, seq_len, num_heads, head_dim, device='cuda', dtype=torch.float16)\n"
"k = torch.randn(batch_size, seq_len, num_heads, head_dim, device='cuda', dtype=torch.float16)\n"
"v = torch.randn(batch_size, seq_len, num_heads, head_dim, device='cuda', dtype=torch.float16)\n"
"\n"
"# Call the fused softmax attention function\n"
"output = flash_attn.flash_attn_func(q, k, v, dropout_p=0.0, softmax_scale=None, causal=False)\n"
"\n"
"print(f\"Output shape: {output.shape}\")\n"
"```\n"
"\n"
"## Key Parameters\n"
"\n"
"- `dropout_p`: Dropout probability. When set to 0.0, dropout is disabled.\n"
"- `softmax_scale`: Scaling factor for softmax. When set to None, it defaults to 1/√d.\n"
"- `causal`: Whether to use causal masking. When set to True, it applies causal masking to the attention scores.\n"
"\n"
"## Performance Comparison\n"
"\n"
"The fused softmax kernel typically achieves a 2-4x speedup compared to the naive implementation. This speedup mainly comes from:\n"
"\n"
"1. Reduced HBM accesses: Intermediate results are kept in SRAM instead of being written to and read from HBM.\n"
"2. Better parallelism: The fused kernel can better utilize the GPU's parallel computing capabilities.\n"
"3. Reduced kernel launch overhead: Multiple kernel launches are combined into a single kernel launch.\n"
"\n"
"## Notes\n"
"\n"
"1. The fused softmax kernel requires the input tensor to be in half precision (FP16) or bfloat16 format.\n"
"2. The input tensor must be contiguous in memory.\n"
"3. The last dimension of the input tensor must be `head_dim`, and `head_dim` must be a multiple of 8.\n"
"4. When using causal masking, the mask is applied to the attention scores before the softmax computation.\n"

msgid "融合 Softmax （Fused Softmax）"
msgstr "Fused Softmax"

msgid "在本节中，我们将使用 Triton 编写一个融合的 softmax 操作的程序。 在此过程中，你会学习到："
msgstr "In this section, we will use Triton to write a program for a fused softmax operation. Along the way, you will learn:"

msgid "内核融合对于带宽受限操作的优势。"
msgstr "The advantage of kernel fusion for bandwidth-bound operations."

msgid "Triton 中缩减操作。"
msgstr "Reduction operations in Triton."

msgid "使用原生 PyTorch 对 X 逐行进行 Softmax 计算"
msgstr "Performing row-wise Softmax computation on X using native PyTorch."

msgid "import torch\\n"
msgstr "import torch"

msgid "内核融合的目的"
msgstr "The purpose of kernel fusion."

msgid "当在 PyTorch 中以原生方式实现时，计算`y=naive_softmax(x)`需要从 DRAM 中读取 5MN+2M 个元素，并写回 3MN+2M 个元素。显然这是非常低效的；我们更希望使用一个自定义的“融合”内核，它只需读取一次 x，并在芯片上完成所有必要的计算。 这样一来只需读取和写回 2MN 个字节，因此我们可以期望理论上的加速比大约为 4 倍（即 (8MN+4M)/2MN）。"
msgstr "When implemented natively in PyTorch, computing `y=naive_softmax(x)` requires reading 5MN+2M elements from DRAM and writing back 3MN+2M elements. This is clearly very inefficient; we would prefer to use a custom \"fused\" kernel that reads x only once and performs all necessary computations on-chip. This way, only 2MN bytes need to be read and written back, so we can expect a theoretical speedup of approximately 4x (i.e., (8MN+4M)/2MN)."

msgid "`torch.jit.script`旨在自动执行这种“内核融合”，但它仍然远未达到理想状态。"
msgstr "`torch.jit.script` is intended to automatically perform this kind of \"kernel fusion,\" but it is still far from ideal."

msgid "计算内核"
msgstr "Compute kernel."

msgid "softmax 内核工作原理如下：每个计算单元（program）以程序数量为步长加载输入矩阵X的一组行数据，执行归一化处理后，将结果写入输出矩阵Y。 注意：Triton 的一个重要限制是每个块必须具有 2 的幂次数的元素，因此，如果我们要处理任意可能的输入形状，需要在内部「填充」每一行，并确保内存操作正确性。"
msgstr "The softmax kernel works as follows: each compute unit (program) loads a set of rows from the input matrix X with a stride equal to the number of programs, performs normalization, and writes the results to the output matrix Y. Note: An important limitation of Triton is that each block must have a power-of-two number of elements, so if we want to handle arbitrary possible input shapes, we need to internally \"pad\" each row and ensure memory operation correctness."

msgid "@triton.jit\\n"
msgstr "@triton.jit"

msgid "我们可以创建一个辅助函数，该函数能够将核函数及其元参数加入执行队列，以处理任意给定的输入张量。"
msgstr "We can create a helper function that enqueues the kernel function and its metadata parameters into the execution queue, so as to handle any given input tensor."

msgid "kernels = {}\\n"
msgstr "kernels = {}"

msgid "单元测试"
msgstr "Unit test"

msgid "需要在一个具有不规则行和列数的矩阵上测试处理好的内核，此举可以验证Padding机制是否起作用"
msgstr "We need to test the processed kernel on a matrix with irregular numbers of rows and columns, which can verify whether the Padding mechanism is working."

msgid "torch.manual_seed(0)\\n"
msgstr "torch.manual_seed(0)"

msgid "Out:"
msgstr "Out:"

msgid "tensor([[0.0002, 0.0017, 0.0009,  ..., 0.0009, 0.0013, 0.0073],\\n"
msgstr "tensor([[0.0002, 0.0017, 0.0009,  ..., 0.0009, 0.0013, 0.0073],"

msgid "\"The maximum difference between torch and triton is 1.4901161193847656e-08\" 表示Triton和PyTorch的输出结果非常接近，肉眼不可区分。"
msgstr "\"The maximum difference between torch and triton is 1.4901161193847656e-08\" indicates that the outputs of Triton and PyTorch are very close and indistinguishable to the naked eye."
